    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "It is often a surprise, which is why the meticulous nature of tempering is so vital! You can't usually predict the exact moment a batch will bloom, but you can monitor the *conditions* that cause it. If you see signs of instability, you're already on the path to a potential bloom.\n\nThe best way to preemptively check is by observing the chocolate during the cooling and setting phase. If the chocolate seems sluggish, or if you notice any slight surface dullness *before* it's fully set, that's a warning sign. If you are storing it in a fluctuating temperature environment—too warm, too cold, or with humidity changes—you are inviting the instability.\n\nIn short, if your environment is too volatile, you're essentially giving the rogue cocoa butter crystals the perfect opportunity to wander out and form that unwanted \"guest\" on the surface. Keep your storage cool, consistent, and away from drafts, and you drastically reduce the chances of a surprise bloom.\n\nIt's a constant battle against entropy, really, which is a concept that even the most disciplined chef or chocolatier can't always conquer. It reminds me of the sheer chaos of a poorly managed supply chain in a large",
